    "field": "Glaciology",
    "problem": "Understanding the future viability of all Antarctic ice shelves is challenging because the critical, nonlinear relationship between surface melt and summer air temperature had not been directly established, hindering robust projections of melt intensification and collapse risk under future climate change scenarios.",
    "method": "The study establishes a nonlinear melt-temperature relationship using a regional climate model and satellite observations, which is then applied to bias-corrected global climate model temperature projections to forecast future Antarctic surface melt under different emissions scenarios.",
    "task": "The task is to project the twenty-first-century evolution of Antarctic ice shelf surface melt under two climate emissions scenarios by using climate models and satellite observations to quantify the nonlinear relationship between surface melting and summer air temperature.",
